1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,946
By the end of this guide, you will know what really makes a skate bearing fast, and how to choose the right set for your level.

2
00:00:07,946 --> 00:00:11,700
You will also learn why a brand new set can still feel slow.

3
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:15,328
Bearings are the cheapest way to make a fast skate faster.

4
00:00:15,328 --> 00:00:18,081
They sit between your wheels and your axles.

5
00:00:18,081 --> 00:00:24,463
They decide how much of your push turns into forward speed, and how much is lost as friction and heat.

6
00:00:24,463 --> 00:00:29,093
A good set feels like the skate keeps rolling long after you stop pushing.

7
00:00:29,093 --> 00:00:33,473
A tired or badly matched set feels like skating uphill on a flat road.

8
00:00:34,173 --> 00:00:38,589
Four things matter far more than the number printed on the shield.

9
00:00:38,589 --> 00:00:40,865
First, the precision of the races.

10
00:00:40,865 --> 00:00:44,411
These are the inner and outer rings the balls run on.

11
00:00:44,411 --> 00:00:49,631
The rounder and smoother they are, the less energy you lose on every rotation.

12
00:00:49,631 --> 00:00:53,311
This is where the real money goes in a premium bearing.

13
00:00:53,311 --> 00:00:54,516
Second, the balls.

14
00:00:54,516 --> 00:00:56,925
Steel balls are tough and forgiving.

15
00:00:56,925 --> 00:01:03,482
Ceramic balls are harder, lighter and rounder, so they run cooler and hold their speed for longer.

16
00:01:03,482 --> 00:01:04,888
Third, the lubricant.

17
00:01:04,888 --> 00:01:07,364
Thick grease protects well but drags.

18
00:01:07,364 --> 00:01:10,174
Light oil is fast, but it needs attention.

19
00:01:10,174 --> 00:01:11,914
Fourth, shields and seals.

20
00:01:11,914 --> 00:01:18,338
Rubber seals keep dirt and water out, but they touch the inner race and cost you a little speed.

21
00:01:18,338 --> 00:01:22,687
Metal or removable shields are faster, but they let more grit in.

22
00:01:23,388 --> 00:01:26,752
Here is what sits inside a standard 608 bearing.

23
00:01:26,752 --> 00:01:32,149
The outer race is the big ring on the outside, twenty two millimetres across.

24
00:01:32,149 --> 00:01:36,635
The inner race sits on your axle, with an eight millimetre bore.

25
00:01:36,635 --> 00:01:42,102
Between them run the balls, seven in most sets and six in the six ball models.

26
00:01:42,102 --> 00:01:45,256
A nylon cage keeps those balls evenly spaced.

27
00:01:45,256 --> 00:01:51,074
A shield or seal covers each side, and the whole bearing is seven millimetres wide.

28
00:01:51,074 --> 00:01:52,686
The key idea is simple.

29
00:01:52,686 --> 00:01:55,420
Speed lives in the races and the balls.

30
00:01:55,420 --> 00:02:00,186
And grit getting between a ball and a race is what kills that speed.

31
00:02:00,886 --> 00:02:04,856
You may have noticed that ABEC rating was not on that list.

32
00:02:04,856 --> 00:02:07,414
ABEC measures manufacturing tolerance.

33
00:02:07,414 --> 00:02:09,163
It does not measure speed.

34
00:02:09,163 --> 00:02:17,238
It tells you nothing about the ball material, the lubricant, or how the bearing behaves under the side load of a corner.

35
00:02:17,238 --> 00:02:21,276
A well made ABEC 7 will beat a badly made ABEC 9 every time.

36
00:02:21,276 --> 00:02:24,102
So treat ABEC as one clue, not as a score.

37
00:02:24,102 --> 00:02:25,784
The same goes for brands.

38
00:02:25,784 --> 00:02:28,611
The market splits the same way everywhere.

39
00:02:28,611 --> 00:02:36,147
Precision steel sits at the sensible end, ceramic in the middle, and full ceramic with premium races at the top.

40
00:02:36,147 --> 00:02:41,598
Judge any bearing against those four points, not against the badge on the shield.

41
00:02:42,298 --> 00:02:44,565
So should you buy steel or ceramic?

42
00:02:44,565 --> 00:02:52,208
If you are still learning to hold a lap, and your skates live in a bag between sessions, steel is the sensible answer.

43
00:02:52,208 --> 00:02:54,670
It costs less and it survives neglect.

44
00:02:54,670 --> 00:03:01,471
And at your current output, the speed difference is smaller than the difference a better push would make.

45
00:03:01,471 --> 00:03:08,596
If you are racing, training most days, and you already clean and oil your kit, ceramic starts to pay you back.

46
00:03:08,596 --> 00:03:10,604
It runs cooler on long efforts.

47
00:03:10,604 --> 00:03:14,231
It holds speed better through the second half of a race.

48
00:03:14,231 --> 00:03:17,275
And it copes far better with humidity and rain.

49
00:03:17,275 --> 00:03:19,218
Fewer balls is another option.

50
00:03:19,218 --> 00:03:23,687
A six ball bearing has fewer contact points, so it spins more freely.

51
00:03:23,687 --> 00:03:30,294
But each ball carries more load, so it suits a light, smooth skater more than a heavy, crashing style.

52
00:03:30,994 --> 00:03:33,919
This one catches people out all the time.

53
00:03:33,919 --> 00:03:39,127
Most inline speed skates use 608 bearings, with an eight millimetre bore.

54
00:03:39,127 --> 00:03:45,834
Some setups, especially certain frames and quad skates, use 627, with a seven millimetre bore.

55
00:03:45,834 --> 00:03:50,400
The outside sizes are close enough to look identical in a photo.

56
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:52,754
But they will not fit each other.

57
00:03:52,754 --> 00:03:55,537
So before you order, look at your axle.

58
00:03:55,537 --> 00:03:57,748
If you are not sure, ask first.

59
00:03:57,748 --> 00:03:59,746
A returned set helps nobody.

60
00:03:59,746 --> 00:04:08,093
And while you are counting, remember that a standard pair of four wheel skates needs sixteen bearings, two per wheel.

61
00:04:08,093 --> 00:04:10,447
A three wheel setup needs twelve.

62
00:04:11,147 --> 00:04:13,538
Here is a story we hear every season.

63
00:04:13,538 --> 00:04:21,290
A skater fits a premium set, rolls out of the door, and the skate feels no faster than the tired bearings that came off.

64
00:04:21,290 --> 00:04:24,844
Nine times out of ten, the bearings are perfectly good.

65
00:04:24,844 --> 00:04:27,557
The fault is in the stack that holds them.

66
00:04:27,557 --> 00:04:33,501
A bearing only spins freely when nothing is squeezing its inner race against its outer race.

67
00:04:33,501 --> 00:04:39,186
Inside each wheel, two bearings sit on the axle with a spacer between their inner races.

68
00:04:39,186 --> 00:04:50,039
When you tighten the axle bolt, the load should travel through the frame, the first inner race, the spacer, the second inner race, and into the other side of the frame.

69
00:04:50,039 --> 00:04:53,334
The balls and the wheel stay out of that load path.

70
00:04:54,034 --> 00:04:56,745
This diagram shows the same wheel two ways.

71
00:04:56,745 --> 00:05:02,293
In the top stack, the spacer carries the clamping load from one frame wall to the other.

72
00:05:02,293 --> 00:05:04,814
The balls stay free and the wheel rolls.

73
00:05:04,814 --> 00:05:10,362
In the bottom stack, the spacer is missing, or too short, or the axle is over tightened.

74
00:05:10,362 --> 00:05:13,956
Now the bolt pulls the two inner races toward each other.

75
00:05:13,956 --> 00:05:18,053
The balls get clamped at an angle they were never built to carry.

76
00:05:18,053 --> 00:05:21,647
The wheel still turns, but against a permanent side load.

77
00:05:21,647 --> 00:05:25,681
It will sound and feel like a worn set from the first kilometre.

78
00:05:25,681 --> 00:05:30,031
Push it hard enough and you can damage the races in a single session.

79
00:05:30,031 --> 00:05:35,642
A premium bearing in the bottom stack will feel slower than a cheap one in the top stack.

80
00:05:36,342 --> 00:05:38,740
Three checks fix almost all of it.

81
00:05:38,740 --> 00:05:46,921
First, make sure every wheel has a spacer, that they are all the same length, and that they match your bearing size.

82
00:05:46,921 --> 00:05:51,294
A 608 spacer in a 627 wheel is a common and expensive mistake.

83
00:05:51,294 --> 00:05:57,148
Second, tighten the axle until the wheel spins freely with no side play, then stop.

84
00:05:57,148 --> 00:05:58,347
Snug, not forced.

85
00:05:58,347 --> 00:06:03,707
If the wheel slows as you make the last quarter turn, you have gone too far.

86
00:06:03,707 --> 00:06:07,445
Third, hold the skate up, spin each wheel and listen.

87
00:06:07,445 --> 00:06:13,228
If one wheel stops earlier than the rest, it is usually the axle, not the bearing.

88
00:06:13,228 --> 00:06:17,813
Run these checks before you spend money on an upgrade, not after.

89
00:06:18,513 --> 00:06:25,054
A cheap bearing that is clean and oiled will comfortably beat an expensive bearing full of grit.

90
00:06:25,054 --> 00:06:26,553
The routine is simple.

91
00:06:26,553 --> 00:06:32,005
Wipe your bearings and the inside of your wheels after any dusty or wet session.

92
00:06:32,005 --> 00:06:33,435
Never leave them wet.

93
00:06:33,435 --> 00:06:39,364
Water sitting inside a bearing overnight does more damage than a month of hard skating.

94
00:06:39,364 --> 00:06:46,110
Strip, clean and oil them when the roll goes noisy or short, rather than waiting for them to seize.

95
00:06:46,110 --> 00:06:52,106
Use a proper skate bearing oil, because household oils are either too thick or too thin.

96
00:06:52,106 --> 00:06:54,150
Ceramic bearings need oil too.

97
00:06:54,150 --> 00:06:57,284
And rotate your wheels so the load stays even.

98
00:06:57,284 --> 00:07:01,441
Looked after like this, a good set lasts seasons, not months.

99
00:07:01,441 --> 00:07:06,551
Many racers also train on steel and keep a ceramic set clean for race days.

100
00:07:07,252 --> 00:07:09,421
Here is the one idea to remember.

101
00:07:09,421 --> 00:07:11,131
Bearings do not add power.

102
00:07:11,131 --> 00:07:13,629
They help you keep the speed you make.

103
00:07:13,629 --> 00:07:19,744
A skater with a good push on steel will beat a skater with a poor push on ceramic every time.

104
00:07:19,744 --> 00:07:25,332
So check your stack, keep your bearings clean, and buy the set that suits your level.

105
00:07:25,332 --> 00:07:32,105
New bearings also feel a little stiff at first, so give them a few easy sessions before you judge them.

106
00:07:32,105 --> 00:07:37,693
The full guide, including the Power Bearings range we stock, is on the LiveLica blog.

107
00:07:37,693 --> 00:07:39,732
The link is in the description.
